---
import { FAQSection, RelatedArticles } from "../../components/article";
import ComparisonTable from "../../components/article/ComparisonTable.astro";
import ArticleLayout from "../../layouts/ArticleLayout.astro";
import { getArticleBySlug } from "../../data/articles";

// Get article metadata from central data file
const article = getArticleBySlug("best-retirement-planning-apps-for-freelancers", "tools")!;
const { title, description, authorSlug, readingTime, image, imageAlt } = article;
const pubDate = new Date(article.pubDate);

// Comparison Table Data
const comparisonColumns = [
  { key: "app", label: "App" },
  { key: "price", label: "Price" },
  { key: "minimum", label: "Minimum" },
  { key: "sepIra", label: "SEP IRA" },
  { key: "taxLoss", label: "Tax-Loss Harvesting" },
];

const comparisonRows = [
  {
    app: "<strong>Betterment</strong>",
    price: "$4/mo or 0.25%/yr",
    minimum: "None",
    sepIra: "Yes",
    taxLoss: "Yes",
  },
  {
    app: "<strong>Wealthfront</strong>",
    price: "0.25%/yr",
    minimum: "$500",
    sepIra: "Yes",
    taxLoss: "Yes",
  },
  {
    app: "<strong>Acorns</strong>",
    price: "$3-5/mo",
    minimum: "None",
    sepIra: "No",
    taxLoss: "No",
  },
];

const headings = [
  { depth: 2, slug: "why-different", text: "Why Freelancer Retirement Is Different" },
  { depth: 2, slug: "betterment", text: "Betterment" },
  { depth: 2, slug: "wealthfront", text: "Wealthfront" },
  { depth: 2, slug: "acorns", text: "Acorns" },
  { depth: 2, slug: "comparison-table", text: "Quick Comparison" },
  { depth: 2, slug: "solo-401k", text: "What About Solo 401(k)?" },
  { depth: 2, slug: "my-setup", text: "My Actual Setup" },
  { depth: 2, slug: "faq", text: "Questions" },
];

const faqData = [
  {
    question: "How much should freelancers save for retirement?",
    answer:
      "The standard advice is 15% of income. But freelancers need to save more since there's no employer match. I aim for 20% minimum. Some months I hit 25% when cash flow allows.",
  },
  {
    question: "Can I still contribute if my income varies wildly?",
    answer:
      "Yes. With SEP IRAs, you contribute based on what you actually earn. Low income month? Contribute less. Big month? Max it out. The limit is 25% of net self-employment income up to 69,000 for 2024.",
  },
  {
    question: "What if I can only afford small amounts?",
    answer:
      "Start anyway. Even 50 bucks a month builds the habit. Use Acorns initially if you need the round-up feature, but graduate to Betterment once you're past 1k or so.",
  },
  {
    question: "SEP IRA or Roth IRA?",
    answer:
      "SEP IRA contributions are pre-tax, reducing current taxes. Roth contributions are after-tax, but withdrawals are tax-free in retirement. If you're in a lower tax bracket now, Roth often makes sense. Higher bracket? SEP. I do both, prioritizing SEP when income is high.",
  },
  {
    question: "What about Bitcoin and crypto for retirement?",
    answer:
      "I'd keep crypto under 5% of retirement savings. Maybe 10% if you're young and aggressive. These platforms offer traditional diversified portfolios, which is what most of your retirement money should be in.",
  },
  {
    question: "Is Betterment worth the monthly fee?",
    answer:
      "At small balances, the 4 dollar monthly fee hurts. But once you're past 20k or set up 250 monthly deposits, you switch to 0.25% annually. At that point, the tax-loss harvesting and RetireGuide features easily pay for themselves.",
  },
];

const relatedArticles = [
  {
    title: "Best Budgeting Apps for Remote Workers",
    excerpt: "Track where your money goes before you save it.",
    href: "/tools/best-budgeting-apps-for-remote-workers",
    image: "/images/unsplash/photo-1526304640581-d334cdbbf45e-1200w.webp",
    imageAlt: "Budgeting apps for remote workers",
  },
  {
    title: "Wise vs PayPal vs Payoneer",
    excerpt: "Compare international payment platforms for freelancers.",
    href: "/tools/wise-vs-paypal-vs-payoneer",
    image: "/images/unsplash/photo-1580894894513-541e068a3e2b-800w.webp",
    imageAlt: "International money transfer and payment apps",
  },
  {
    title: "Tax Tips for Remote Workers",
    excerpt: "Maximize deductions as a remote worker or digital nomad.",
    href: "/blog/tax-tips-remote-workers",
    image: "/images/unsplash/photo-1554224155-6726b3ff858f-800w.webp",
    imageAlt: "Calculator and financial documents for tax planning",
  },
];
---

<ArticleLayout
    title={title}
    description={description}
    authorSlug={authorSlug}
    pubDate={pubDate}
    readingTime={readingTime}
    image={image}
    imageAlt={imageAlt}
    category="Finance"
    categorySlug="tools"
    subcategorySlug="finance"
    headings={headings}
>
  <p class="lead">
    The first time I tried to set up retirement savings as a freelancer, I nearly gave up. No HR
    department to auto-enroll me. No employer match. Just me, a pile of confusing options, and the
    creeping fear that I'd be working until I was 80.
  </p>

  <p>
    Traditional 401(k) providers wanted nothing to do with my irregular income. Banks pushed
    products with fees that would eat my savings alive. I spent three months paralyzed by options
    before finally just picking something. That something cost me around 1,800 in unnecessary fees
    over two years.
  </p>

  <p>
    The good news: it's way easier now. Robo-advisors have gotten actually good at handling
    freelancer retirement. They understand irregular income. They offer SEP IRAs. They don't
    require you to have a finance degree.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>Short version if you're in a hurry:</strong> Betterment for most freelancers. Low fees,
    SEP IRA option, and their RetireGuide tool actually understands freelancer finances. Wealthfront
    if you want more hands-off automation. Acorns only if you're just starting out with spare change.
  </p>

  <h2 id="why-different">Why Freelancer Retirement Is Different</h2>

  <p>Regular employees have it easy. Money gets pulled from their paycheck before they see it.
    Employer matches feel like free money. Automatic increases happen without thinking.</p>

  <p>Freelancers get none of that.</p>

  <p>
    You pay both halves of Social Security. Your income varies month to month. You need to actively
    choose to set money aside. And the options are more complicated. Traditional IRA, Roth IRA,
    SEP IRA, Solo 401(k). Each has different contribution limits and tax implications.
  </p>

  <p>
    The contribution limits are actually better for freelancers. SEP IRAs let you put away up to
    25% of net self-employment income, maxing at 69,000 for 2024. Compare that to the 23,000 limit
    on a regular 401(k). The opportunity is there. You just need the right tools.
  </p>

  <h2 id="betterment">Betterment</h2>

  <p>This is what I use. Have been for about three years now.</p>

  <p>
    Betterment gets freelancers. They offer SEP IRAs, which most robo-advisors skip. Their
    RetireGuide tool pulls in all your accounts, including that old 401(k) you forgot about, and
    shows you if you're on track. It's not just numbers in a vacuum. It accounts for irregular income.
  </p>

  <p>
    The fee structure changed recently. You either pay 4 bucks a month or 0.25% annually if you
    have 20k or set up recurring deposits of 250 monthly. For most freelancers, the annual
    percentage works out better once you're past about 20k invested.
  </p>

  <h3>What I actually use it for</h3>

  <p>
    Automatic deposits when income comes in. I set up transfers for 15% of every client payment.
    Tax-loss harvesting happens automatically, which saved me around 400 last year in taxes. Their
    goal-based projections show me when I can realistically retire based on actual numbers, not
    fantasies.
  </p>

  <h3>The good stuff</h3>

  <p>
    No minimum to start. SEP IRA available. Tax-coordinated portfolio across all your accounts.
    RetireGuide actually useful. The app doesn't overwhelm you with options. Human advisor access
    if you pay more.
  </p>

  <h3>What's annoying</h3>

  <p>
    The 4 dollar monthly fee hits hard when you're just starting with small balances. Interface
    could be simpler. Premium tier with human advisors costs 0.65% plus 299 yearly, which adds up fast.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>Betterment is best for:</strong> Freelancers serious about retirement who want hands-off
    investing with solid tax optimization.
  </p>

  <h2 id="wealthfront">Wealthfront</h2>

  <p>
    Wealthfront takes the opposite approach from Betterment. Fully automated. No human advisors.
    Just algorithms doing their thing.
  </p>

  <p>
    Their Path tool is genuinely impressive for planning. It connects to all your accounts and runs
    scenarios. What if you retire at 55? What if the market tanks? What if you move somewhere
    cheaper? All modeled out.
  </p>

  <p>
    The catch: you need 500 to start. For a freelancer just getting going, that's a real barrier.
    But once you're past that threshold, the experience is smooth.
  </p>

  <h3>How it works</h3>

  <p>
    Connect your accounts. Answer questions about your goals. Wealthfront builds a diversified
    portfolio across ETFs. Tax-loss harvesting kicks in automatically. You basically forget about
    it until you need the money.
  </p>

  <h3>The good stuff</h3>

  <p>
    Excellent tax optimization, including direct indexing for larger accounts. Path planning tool
    is best in class. 0.25% annual fee is straightforward. Good variety of account types including
    Roth IRA, traditional IRA, SEP IRA.
  </p>

  <h3>What's annoying</h3>

  <p>
    500 minimum is a barrier for beginners. No human advisors ever. The automation means less
    flexibility. Their SEP IRA offering is less prominent than Betterment's.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>Wealthfront is best for:</strong> Freelancers with steady income who want fully
    automated investing and don't need to talk to humans.
  </p>

  <h2 id="acorns">Acorns</h2>

  <p>Acorns is the starter option. Not necessarily for serious retirement planning, but for building the habit.</p>

  <p>
    The idea is simple. Round up your purchases and invest the difference. Buy a 3.50 coffee,
    round up to 4, invest the 50 cents. It sounds trivial, and honestly, it kind of is. But for
    people who've never invested anything, it removes the mental barrier.
  </p>

  <p>
    The problem: at 3 bucks a month, those fees hurt when you're only investing spare change. If
    you invest 20 bucks through round-ups, you're paying 15% in fees that month. That's brutal.
  </p>

  <h3>What it's actually good for</h3>

  <p>
    Getting started. Building the habit of seeing investment accounts grow. Learning what a
    portfolio looks like. Then graduating to something with lower percentage fees once you have
    real money to invest.
  </p>

  <h3>The good stuff</h3>

  <p>
    No minimum investment. Retirement account option included in Personal tier. The round-up
    feature is genuinely clever. Simple enough that anyone can use it.
  </p>

  <h3>What's annoying</h3>

  <p>
    3 to 5 dollar monthly fee is expensive for small balances. No tax-loss harvesting. No SEP IRA
    for freelancers. Limited customization. You'll outgrow it fast.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>Acorns is best for:</strong> Complete beginners who need to build the investing habit
    before they're ready for serious retirement planning.
  </p>

  <h2 id="comparison-table">Quick Comparison</h2>

  <ComparisonTable columns={comparisonColumns} rows={comparisonRows} />

  <h2 id="solo-401k">What About Solo 401(k)?</h2>

  <p>
    This is the other option freelancers hear about. Solo 401(k) plans let you contribute even more
    than SEP IRAs in some cases, and you can do both employer and employee contributions.
  </p>

  <p>
    The downside: more paperwork. Annual filings once you hit 250k. More complexity in general.
  </p>

  <p>
    None of the robo-advisors handle Solo 401(k) well. If you want that route, you're looking at
    Fidelity, Schwab, or Vanguard directly. More work, but potentially more tax-advantaged space.
  </p>

  <p>For most freelancers, a SEP IRA through Betterment or Wealthfront is simpler and good enough.</p>

  <h2 id="my-setup">My Actual Setup</h2>

  <p>Here's exactly what I do.</p>

  <p>
    Client payment comes in. 15% goes to Betterment automatically. Within Betterment, my SEP IRA
    gets priority contributions up to the limit. The rest goes to a taxable brokerage account.
    Tax-loss harvesting happens in the background.
  </p>

  <p>
    Every quarter, I check the RetireGuide projections. Adjust contribution percentage if income
    changed significantly. Takes maybe 20 minutes a quarter.
  </p>

  <p>
    I kept my old employer 401(k) at Fidelity because the fees are low and rolling it over wasn't
    worth the hassle. Betterment's RetireGuide still factors it into my projections.
  </p>

  <h2 id="faq">Questions</h2>

  <FAQSection faqs={faqData}/>

  <h2>Recommendations</h2>

  <p>Three years of freelancing. Four different apps tested. Way too much time on retirement forums. So:</p>

  <p>
    <strong>Betterment</strong> if you're serious about freelancer retirement. The SEP IRA, tax
    optimization, and RetireGuide make it the best overall choice. I use it. It works.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>Wealthfront</strong> if you want maximum automation and already have 500 to start.
    Their planning tools are slightly better. No human support though.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>Acorns</strong> only as a stepping stone. Great for building habits. Bad for actual
    long-term retirement savings due to fees.
  </p>

  <p>
    The best retirement app is the one you'll actually use. But at some point you need to graduate
    from round-ups to real contributions. Start with whatever gets you going, but have a plan to
    move to Betterment or Wealthfront once you're past the beginner phase.
  </p>

  <p>Future you will thank current you. Or curse you. Depends on whether you read this and did nothing.</p>

  <Fragment slot="full-width">
    <RelatedArticles articles={relatedArticles}/>
  </Fragment>
</ArticleLayout>
